README.OSK -- K5JB's KA9Q NET, version k37, and BM 3.3.1n4, on OS-9/68000WHAT THIS IS  NET is Phil Karn's KA9Q Internet Protocol Package -- TCP/IP over SLIP,  KISS and AX.25 packet radio, with telnet, ftp, smtp, finger and a  NET/ROM node -- as Joe Buswell, K5JB, maintained it for Unix, with the  OS-9/68000 port by Robert E. Brose II, N0QBJ, folded into his kit.  BM is  Bdale Garbee's mailer from the same kit: it writes letters into the queue  NET's SMTP client sends from, and reads the mailbox NET's SMTP server  delivers to.  k37 is K5JB's last release (README here says so), and the  N0QBJ port is the one in it, so NET and BM are built from the same source  as the binaries they replace were.WHERE IT CAME FROM  The k37 kit as K5JB uploaded it, from the git repository  https://github.com/johnsonjh/k5jb.git, commit  124f2c1c50c65224d600ef3cf2827dd58fc2d52c ("k5jb k37", 2024-03-16), which  is a single-commit import of the kit.  ORIG/ is README, k5jb/ and bm/  from that commit, byte for byte except that line ends are CR, as every  text file on this disk is.  The kit's doc/ (Bdale Garbee's manual,  chapters 0-8, which NET's own start-up message sends you to as "Chapter  2") and samp/ (K5JB's sample startup.net, hosts.net, help files) are in  DOC/net and DOC/net/samp, unchanged.WHAT SHIPS  CMDS/NETWORK/net   built from k5jb/ by tools/rebuild/recipes.psv  CMDS/NETWORK/bm    built from bm/, the same way  Both are built -qm and need none of Microware's run-time modules.CHANGES -- three source edits and one added file, each for the build  k5jb/sys/stat.h     ADDED, empty.  note.os9 (N0QBJ) says to make an empty                      SYS/stat.h "to minimize the number of changes to the                      source"; it goes here rather than in the compiler's                      DEFS.  k5jb/options.h      #define CUTE_VIDEO is inside #ifndef _OSK.  On Unix                      it uses terminfo -- <term.h> and setupterm() -- which                      OS-9 has not got; Makefile.os9 says "if you don't have                      curses, forget the whole thing".  Without it incoming                      sessions are shown in plain video, as the earlier OS-9                      build showed them.  k5jb/osk_io.c       its own fputc() is inside #ifndef HAVE_FPUTC, and the                      recipe defines HAVE_FPUTC.  The C library used here                      has fputc, and l68 stops on the duplicate.  bm/main.c           check_maildir() gets `DIR *md;'.  Its _OSK branch uses                      md, declared only as a local of main(), so it did not                      compile; the branch was added after the OS-9 port                      ("added n3") and had not been compiled on OS-9.  Defines, from Makefile.os9 plus _OSK:    net  UNIX MSPTICK=10 remote=rremote _OSK HAVE_FPUTC    bm   UNIX _OSK  _OSK is not predefined by the compiler used here (it defines OSK), and  both programs keep all their OS-9 code under _OSK.  remote=rremote is  N0QBJ's: `remote' is a storage class in Microware C.  And one change that is not for the build: the date in a queued letter's  Date: header (bm/send.c) and in NET's Received: line (k5jb/smtpserv.c)  was written from tm_year with %02d, so after 1999 it read "24 Sep 126" --  not a date a mail program anywhere can parse.  Both now write  tm_year % 100, so it reads "24 Sep 26", as RFC 822 has it.  Nothing else  was changed.SETTING IT UP  Both programs find their files through two environment variables:    NETHOME   startup.net, hosts.net, ftpusers, aliases, helpbox.hlp,              net.hlp, and BM's .bmrc  (unset: your home directory)    NETSPOOL  mail/, mqueue/ and rqueue/ below it  (unset: /usr/spool,              which OS-9 cannot have -- set it)  N0QBJ's note.os9 also sets TMPDIR (he used a RAM disk).  DOC/net/samp has  K5JB's samples, and DOC/net/chapter2 is the setup chapter.  NET's serial  interfaces want the port set up with xmode first (8 bits, 1 stop bit);  the baud-rate field of `attach' is not used on OS-9.  A .bmrc needs at least      host <your.host.name>      user <you>  and may add fullname, reply, editor, maxlet and the rest (chapter 4).RUNNING IT  net                 reads NETHOME/startup.net, then gives a NET> prompt.                      `?' lists the commands.  `exit please' leaves it                      (plain `exit' is refused once servers are running).  bm addr ...         compose a letter to each address; `.' alone on a                      line ends it.  -s <subject> on the command line.  bm addr < file      the letter is the file; headers are added.  bm -q addr < file   the file is already a whole message; it is queued                      as it is, with no headers added.  bm                  read your mailbox, NETSPOOL/mail/<user>.txt; `?'                      lists the commands.  -u <user> or -f <file> for                      another mailbox.  NET's `!', `dir', `pwd' and the ftp server's mkdir/rmdir run your own  OS-9 commands through its `shell'.TERMS -- in the kit's own words  NET (K5JB kit):   "Internet Protocol Package, (C) 1988 by Phil Karn,                    KA9Q" -- the banner, k5jb/main.c line 1051.  The kit                    carries no licence statement from Karn beyond that.  NET/ROM code:     "Copyright 1989 by Daniel M. Frank, W9NK.  Permission                    granted for non-commercial distribution only."                    k5jb/nr3.c lines 2-3 (and nr4.c, nr4subr.c, nr4timer.c,                    nr4user.c, nrcmd.c, nrsubr.c).  doc/chapter5 (DOC/net/                    chapter5), lines 362-367, adds: "so long as the                    copyright notice is not removed from the source files,                    and the program in which it is used displays 'NET/ROM                    code copyright 1989 by Dan Frank, W9NK' when it starts                    up."  net prints "NET/ROM Support (C) 1989 by Dan                   